<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagoya</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagoya is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aichi-prefecture">Aichi Prefecture</a> and one of the largest cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It grew from the castle built from 1610 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>'s orders, to which the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiyosu">Kiyosu</a> was moved, and served as the seat of the Owari Tokugawa, the senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gosanke">Gosanke</a> house. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-muneharu">Tokugawa Muneharu</a> in the 1730s it became known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and commerce, and it is now a centre of the car and aerospace industries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagoya.jpg" alt="Nagoya" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ウィキ太郎(WikiTaro) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nagoya_Station_-_View_from_the_Main_Building_in_Nagoya_Campus_of_Aichi_University_2022-6-29.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nagoya lies on the Pacific coast of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-bay">Ise Bay</a> on the Nōbi Plain, one of the most fertile areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The city was built on low plateaus to keep off floodwaters. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiso-river">Kiso River</a> runs along its western border, and the Shōnai River comes from the northeast and turns south towards the bay. The orderly grid of its streets is ultimately credited to the shogun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> and his protégés <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> were warlords based in the Nagoya area who gradually unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. In 1610 Ieyasu moved the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiyosu">Kiyosu</a>, about seven kilometres away, to a more strategic site in what is now Nagoya. Nagoya Castle was built partly from materials taken from Kiyosu Castle, and the whole town around it, some 60,000 people, moved with it. Nearby Atsuta Shrine became a waystation called Miya on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tōkaidō</a> road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and Edo, and a town grew up to serve travellers. The castle town and the shrine town together formed the city.</p><p>Nagoya was a major trading city and the seat of the Owari lords, the most important house of the Tokugawa clan. They encouraged trade and the arts, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-muneharu">Tokugawa Muneharu</a>, the seventh lord, who took a keen interest in drama and lived lavishly; actors began to visit the city, and wealthy merchants supported the arts further. Nō and Kyōgen theatre date back to the Owari lords, and the Misono-za, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabuki">kabuki</a> stages, developed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>.</p><p>In the early 1900s a convergence of economic factors brought rapid growth. The area's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobile</a>, aviation and shipbuilding industries flourished, following older crafts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">bicycles</a>, sewing machines and timepieces, and then ceramics, chemicals, oil, petrochemicals and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>. These industries made Nagoya a target of American air raids in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a>, when the Mitsubishi A6M Zero fighter was built there, and many old buildings and artefacts were destroyed. The city had 153,370 households at the war's end in 1945, and it rebuilt as a leading centre of industry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nagoya is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aichi-prefecture">Aichi Prefecture</a> and the largest city of the Chūbu region, with about 2.3 million people. It is the principal city of the Chūkyō metropolitan area, and its seaport is the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It is linked to Tokyo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> by the Tōkaidō Shinkansen. Atsuta Shrine and Nagoya Castle are its two best-known sights, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> recognises it as a Design City.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nagoya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1610 Ieyasu ordered lords across Japan to build <b>Nagoya</b> castle, and the whole town of Kiyosu was moved there, creating the city of Nagoya.</blockquote><a href="/state/owari-domain">Owari Domain</a></li></ul></section>
